{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>If there be a prophet among you</b> — If there be to you a prophet, according to the words of Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 12:6). And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that its meaning is: if your prophet be a prophet of YHWH, as in \"and the prophecy of Oded the prophet\" (II Chronicles 15:8). And well did he speak. And the meaning of the verse is that even if your prophet be a prophet of YHWH, he does not prophesy by My great Name but only in a vision or in riddles. And Scripture mentioned this because many of the prophets do not attain to this, but they are prophets through ru'aḥ ha-kodesh (the holy spirit), in the manner of what is said, \"the ru'aḥ of YHWH spoke through me\" (II Samuel 23:2), and this is \"the hand of YHWH\" spoken of in Ezekiel, and it will be explained in the words of Zechariah." "<b>In a vision do I make Myself known to him</b> — Scripture did not say <b>in a vision do I show Myself to him</b>, but said <b>I make Myself known</b>. And behold, this verse is like the verse \"and I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob as El Shaddai\" (Exodus 6:3). It says that the great Name will be in the vision, and through it He makes Himself known to the prophet, not by His great Name, as He said (there), \"but My name YHWH I did not make known to them.\" And He said that the speech will be in a dream, but not so My servant Moses, for in all My house through which the prophets see dreams, he is faithful and knows on his own all the attributes, and mouth to mouth will be his speech from Me, and the temunah he beholds — he does not see it in a dream. And the language of Sifrei (BeHa'alotekha 103): \"'and the temunat of YHWH he beholds' — this is a vision from behind.\" And behold, here Scripture teaches the difference between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of the others who were in his generation, and He already mentioned this in the verses preceding it, \"and I appeared to Abraham\" etc., as I explained, and He will mention it likewise at the end of the Torah concerning those who come after him: \"and there arose not a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom YHWH knew face to face\" (Deuteronomy 34:10). And the meaning in all of them is identical. And do not be troubled by what they said concerning Samuel, that he was equal to Moses, for it is written \"though Moses and Samuel stood before Me, My mind could not be toward this people\" (Jeremiah 15:1), for because He mentioned him together with him, our Sages counted him for greatness, not that they would equate their prophecies, heaven forbid. And the meaning of the verse is that He mentioned Moses because of his standing before Him in the breach to turn back His wrath from destruction, both in the golden calf and in the spies, who were liable to annihilation, and He mentioned Samuel because this verse concerns the matter of the famines, when they needed rain, saying \"are there among the vanities of the nations any that give rain?\" etc. (Jeremiah 14:22). And HaShem said that if Samuel, who brings down rain out of its season in the days of wheat harvest (I Samuel 12:17), were to stand before Him, He would not listen to him to bring it down for them in the time of their distress. And behold, He mentioned the great ones of the tribe whom HaShem chose to minister to Him and to bless in His name, who pray on behalf of Israel. And so too the meaning in what is said: \"Moses and Aaron among His priests, and Samuel among those who call upon His name\" etc. (Psalms 99:6)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:49.019521528Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}